block.text:
Confirmed: "`execute` runs immediately; the disposers it produces are collected and run (in reverse order) either when the returned disposer is called or when the fiber unloads".

So `ctx.effect(cb)` runs `cb` immediately, and whatever `cb` RETURNS (a disposer) is the cleanup that runs on disposal/unload.

My code: `ctx.effect(() => dispose())` — the callback runs immediately and calls `dispose()` right away, which unregisters the tool! And the return value of `dispose()` (undefined, probably) is registered as cleanup. So the tool got registered in apply, then immediately unregistered when the effect ran.

The correct pattern is `ctx.effect(() => dispose)` — the callback returns the disposer without calling it. Let me verify with an existing example of a registration: e.g., `ctx.effect(() => ctx.webServer.register(route), ...)` — that works because `webServer.register(route)` returns the disposer; the effect body runs immediately and returns the disposer to be collected. So registering the tool: `ctx.effect(() => harness.registerTool(ctx, tool))` — the body runs immediately, registers the tool, and returns the registry disposer which is collected for cleanup.

Alternatively, my current code: `const dispose = harness.registerTool(ctx, tool)` registers; then `ctx.effect(() => dispose())` — BAD: calls dispose immediately. Fix: `ctx.effect(() => dispose)`.

Actually even simpler and idiomatic: `ctx.effect(() => harness.registerTool(ctx, tool), 'greet tool registration')`.

So the fix: define a new Package (pkg-2) under grx-1 with the corrected host code, then update to it.

Wait — but hold on. Do I even need the ctx.effect wrapper? The run lifecycle: does it call the fiber's disposers when the plugin stops? The run lifecycle stops the plugin by unloading the fiber, which runs collected disposers. The `tools.register` disposer unregisters the tool. Without ctx.effect, the registration wouldn't be cleaned up on stop — the tool would remain registered after the plugin stops (a leak). So yes, keeping `ctx.effect(() => dispose)` is the right approach per the guidelines.

Let me define pkg-2 with corrected code:

```js
return {
  name: 'greet-tool',
  inject: ['tools'],
  apply(ctx) {
    const tool = harness.defineTool({
      name: 'greet',
      description: 'Greets a person by name, returning "hello <name>".',
      parameters: { name: { type: 'string', required: true } },
      output: {
        schema: { type: 'string' },
        render: (_args, value) => [{ type: 'text', text: value }],
      },
      async execute(args) {
        return 'hello ' + args.name
      },
    })
    ctx.effect(() => harness.registerTool(ctx, tool))
  },
}
```

Then cordis_run update grx-1 pkg-2.

After that, verify via Tool.listTools that `greet` appears. Then call the greet tool with name "world" — it should appear in my toolset on the next model step. Since the tool will be in my toolset after the run, I can invoke it as a tool call in my next turn.

Let me define the corrected package now.

